Since $X$ is Hadamard, $x\mapsto \frac{1}{2}d(x,y)^2$ is 1-strongly convex for any $y\in C$.
It follows that for all
$\delta \geq s t$ we have
\begin{align*}
	d(x^{+},y)^2 &= d\left(P_C(r\hspace{-0.05cm}\left(st\right)),P_C(y)\right)^2\\
	&\leq d\left(r\hspace{-0.05cm}\left(st\right),y\right)^2\\
	&\leq \left(1-\frac{s t}{\delta}\right)d(x,y)^2 + \frac{s t}{\delta}
	d(r(\delta),y)^2 - \left(1 - \frac{s t}{\delta}\right)\frac{s t}{\delta}
	d(x,r(\delta))^2\\
	&= \left(1-\frac{s t}{\delta}\right)d(x,y)^2 + \frac{s t}{\delta}
	d(r(\delta),y)^2 + s^2t^2 - \delta s t\\
	&= \left(1-\frac{s t}{\delta}\right)d(x,y)^2 + \frac{s t}{\delta}(
d(r(\delta),y)^2 - \delta^2) + 
s^2t^2.
\end{align*}
The second line uses nonexpansivity of the projection $P_C$, see \cite[Theorem 2.1.12]{bacakbook}.

The last statement of the theorem can be proven in exactly the same way
			 as \cite[Theorem 8.35(a)]{beckfirstorder}.
\end{proof}
